; slack_empty_capacity_int_register: SLACK discriminates both of its
; registers even when the capacity is non-positive and nothing is appended.
;
; SLACK carries a `mutate` effect on both registers, and the verifier's
; static type system depends on it: xqvm/src/verifier/reg_type.rs requires
; vec<int> of each, and RegType::Any -- the write type INPUT carries --
; satisfies that requirement. So this program is verifier-clean and
; therefore admissible to a chain's store_program, and it arrives at SLACK
; with two int registers.
;
; Returning Continue on `capacity <= 0` before touching either register
; would let the program halt and write outputs; the VM must raise
; TypeMismatch. Same class, and same rule, as ITER's
; register-read-before-skip.
;
; The registers come from calldata rather than STOW for the same reason
; iter_non_vec_register_empty_slice uses INPUT: it is the shape the
; verifier admits.

PUSH 0
INPUT r0         ; r0 = 5, an int, not a vec<int>
PUSH 1
INPUT r1         ; r1 = 5, an int, not a vec<int>

PUSH 0           ; start_index
PUSH 0           ; capacity -- non-positive, appends nothing
SLACK r0 r1

PUSH 7
STOW r2
PUSH 0
OUTPUT r2
HALT
